now throughout the pandemic intensive  care has been a part of the nhs which  has been under the most consistent  pressure  now doctors are warning that some staff  simply don't want to come back to work  because of the huge demands being placed  on them  well doctor shonda pondlar who's an  intensive care consultant uh is also  honorary secretary of the intensive care  society joins us now  um you've described it as being a little  bit like working  in a war zone just just tell us what the  daily grind  is like at the moment for those in  intensive care  hi um good morning jane um  first of all it's we're starting  this has been going on for about 10  months now so  we've been seeing increased numbers of  patients coming in  over a variety of ages many of them in  their 40s 50s 60s  who were previously fit and well who are  incredibly sick intensive care units  across the country have been doubled in  numbers  um so the number of normal beds that  they had a year ago  they've been running it twice that for  several months  of course we don't have those kind of  staffing available  so we've had to redeploy staff from  other areas and current staff  have been working extras when there's  meant to be  resting or when they're meant to be on  holiday  um and on top of that we've seen a  significant rise  in the number of people being off sick  and a lot of that is just due to the  pressure of the work  because often these staff won't  necessarily be intensive care staff so  they're  they're being put in in an environment  which is a great deal more stressful  than they're used to  at a time when it's even more stressful  than it normally would be  absolutely and what we're also finding  is that many intensive care units  um there are a lot of patients turning  up that would normally be treated on  intensive care but are having the same  treatments  outside of intensive care and the  respiratory doctors and the acute  physicians  are all having to look after those  patients so the nurses in those areas  who are having to be trained up to use  them in very very  rapid time  uh understandably uh feeling  under pressure um so across the country  we're seeing the staff  are getting to the point now where  they're really thinking we can't how  much longer do we have to do this for  what  what kind of stuff are you having to do  for patients because  at the end of their lives they'd  obviously hope to have their loved ones  around them and that's just not possible  because of covert  absolutely so what normally happens is  to  their families have been visiting  patients and i  and if we feel that they are  aren't likely to survive we will call  the fa we will  call the families in go and sit down  chat to them make sure that they  have a nursing staff that they may well  have become quite friendly with  um to to comfort them as well and  then they can go in and sit with their  loved one and watch them pass away now  this time round the last thing many  families will remember  is seeing their loved one being bundled  away in the back of an ambulance  and us giving  daily updates on what's going on but the  interventions we do on intensive care  are very  challenging to actually visualize and so  often  we find ourselves having to have that  conversation at the  end of life um quite often places will  allow families to come in at that point  but again if a family is having to  isolate then  that's not a viable option and many of  them live  too remote to be able to do that that  we find ourselves taking the place of  those families and  ensuring the the patient's  wishes regarding uh how they pass away  uh followed so that's  in terms of just actually physically  being there maybe playing music  maybe just just them knowing that  they're not alone  yes i've had colleagues taking beer for  example  i've had colleagues play guitar  whatever we can do that is within our  capacity we will we will do  often it's putting the family on on  facetime  and allowing them to be able to converse  or even just look at the patient passing  away in the hope that that gives some  sort of a demonstrated love for them  i mean having to break the news to a  family that their loved one  is about to pass away must be absolutely  the hardest and worst part of your job  how many times a day are you having to  do it  well i think normally we'd probably do  it maybe once a day  and over the last few months maybe  anywhere up to two three times a day  i mean and it becomes emotion it's very  emotionally draining  for everyone involved it's very  difficult to keep  doing that i mean the families  obviously must express their gratitude  do you feel  their gratitude from them and the rest  of the population for what you guys are  are doing it's truly extraordinary  i i am yes i think the the families  definitely  uh know and and do thank us afterwards  um again sometimes it's very hard to  understand what's happening  so sometimes it can be very frustrating  for for both sides  and the public as well you know on the  whole they they do show  gratitude for what's going on and they  do  um honor our requests to stay away from  the hospital  first of all because we don't we don't  um want to encourage people who've been  isolating to  spread the disease but um  you know it's very frustrating for them  to not be able to visit for example  and i know a lot of families really  struggle with that and i'm very grateful  that they  follow the rules and just briefly how do  you  cope how do you decompress who do you go  to  at the end of a horrific day  well it's it's always your family i find  it very difficult to decompress but um  you know i've got three children and my  wife  spends most of her time when she's not  operating she's a surgeon  um basically uh  corralling my children into quiet areas  to allow me a bit of time um but they're  all being home schooled at the moment  yeah i watched the football or you know  i follow manchester united  um i've got a guitar that i'd play very  badly  um but it's those kind of things it's  the small things that you take comfort  in  you know play with your children build  lego with them watch films  but the difference between i know you  described i've  described as being in a war zone  occasionally it's it's different to  being in a war zone  because in a war zone you go away for  several months and then you come back  and you you anticipate prolonged periods  of peace  in this you go to a very highly stressed  environment where you're making  decisions that have a repercussions if  you get them wrong  for 12 15 hours and then you come home  where that stimulation isn't there and  it's very hard to turn on turn off  and i think a lot of my colleagues that  we're seeing  really struggle with that kind of  intermittent overload  and then come home again doctor  shondapan  we're gonna have to leave there i'm  afraid but we're really grateful for you  for coming on  this morning and talking to us and  indeed grateful for all that you and  your colleagues are doing for so many  families  thanks a lot
 

